\section{Limits and Continuity of Functions}
In this chapter are some basic definitions and theorems from calculus most needed for differential equations.
\begin{definition}
A function $f(x)$ has the \textbf{limit} $L$ as $x$ approaches $a$, in symbols 
\[\lim _{x\rightarrow a} f(x)=L,\] if, for every positive number $\epsilon$ there is a positive number $\delta$, such that if $0<|x-a|<\delta$, then $|f(x)-L|<\epsilon$.
\end{definition}
\begin{definition}
A function $f(x)$ is defined to be \textbf{continuous at the point} $x=a$, if 
\[\lim _{x\rightarrow a} f(x)=f(a).\]
\end{definition}
\section{Derivatives and Integrals}
The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined to be the limit 
\[\lim _{h\rightarrow0}{\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}}=f'(x)=\dfrac{d}{dx}f(x).\] 
Observe that if the derivative exists at $x$, then certainly $f(x)$ is continuous at $x$. The derivative of $y=f(x)$ is usually denoted by $y'=\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.However, in cases where the independent variable is the time $t$, Newton's notation $\dot{y}$ is often used.

\noindent A function may be continuous and yet fail to have a derivative. 
\begin{example}
$y=|x|$ does not have a derivative at $x=0$.
\end{example} 
\begin{definition}
A function $F(x)$ is an \textbf{indefinite integral} of $f(x)$, 
\[F(x)=\int f(x), dx, \] if \[\dfrac{d}{dx}F(x)=f(x).\]
\end{definition}
